# Public Speaking
Tamil title: மேடைப் பேச்சு

In 1948, Arignar Anna explained how public speaking should be shaped.

In our time, public speaking has become an important instrument in the worthy work of governing a country. This instrument does not belong only to a few, nor should it. Public speech has now taken the place once occupied by the crown, the sword and the rosary. As democracy grows, its importance will grow.

As its importance and strength increase, so too may the danger of using it to advance self-interest. A malicious desire to mislead the people may spread. If an instrument of navigation falls into evil hands, the ship strikes a rock. Likewise, if the powerful instrument of public speech, capable of moving governments, is available only to a few and they misuse that power, the people's welfare becomes a ship dashed against the rocks. We must therefore strive to make this power available to large numbers of people.

Those who govern can use public speech to offer reasons for anything. To protect themselves from rulers acting improperly and making them suffer, the people too must take up public speech. Thus, for the two sides—rulers and ruled—it serves at one time as sword, at another as shield; at one time as lamp, at another as torch.

In earlier days, rulers had only certain basic duties: guarding against foreign invasion, internal disorder and wild animals entering settled country. Government did not enter the people's daily lives. Now the position is reversed.

The ruler has authority to measure how much rice a person may eat. The people have the right to say, “Go even to Australia and bring us food; if you cannot, leave the seat of government.” Rulers now occupy a much larger place in private life. The people's responsibility to monitor the rulers' nature, purpose and plans has therefore increased. Public speech is important for this work of vigilance.

But can it be done? Can we do it? Such doubts may arise.

Human beings saw birds circling in the sky, marvelled, and began to think that a device capable of similar flight would be immensely useful. That thought gave us the aeroplane.

Those who built wooden vessels to master the billowing sea, craft that travel beneath the sea, and machines that fly in the sky; those who invented instruments to hear and see things far away; those who brought wild rivers under control and discovered the power of fire within water—all were human beings like us. The thought “If they can do it, so can we” became the force that encouraged them.

When all those things are possible, can public speaking be impossible? Remaining silent may be difficult; it is hard to suppress the mind and sit still. Speaking is easy. Bringing speech under order and method is public speaking.

The first task, therefore, is to understand that public speaking is necessary and useful today, and to believe that we too can acquire the ability.

Next we must consider how speech should be shaped and used. Should it be a breeze or a storm? Sweet coconut water or bitter neem juice? This question will certainly arise. It is a slippery platform, a place where those preparing themselves for public speaking must plant their feet with great care.

Beginning to study public speaking with the sole aim of speaking sweetly is not only improper but unnecessary. This does not mean harsh speech is preferable. I mean only that one should not make the sole test whether words burn or give pleasure.

Such a method may be possible and useful to one conducting a religious discourse on the marriage of Rukmini, the coronation of Rama or the story of Seemanthani. Milk may be reduced to a quarter of its volume, mixed with powdered rock candy and saffron paste, then served with devotion and desire added because even that is not enough.

But public speaking is not a religious entertainment. It expresses views on living questions open to debate. It is not verbal commerce designed to supply sweetness to everyone. It is not a sermon delivered before people who sit with mouths closed and hands folded. It is not a divine oracle, nor a situation in which the speaker is acknowledged to be wiser than the listeners. It is not verbal music performed because the listeners command, “Speak upon this subject.”

Its work is to clarify issues connected with life when people hold confused ideas, remove their bewilderment when they are alarmed, and make them understand responsibility for a question when they are unconcerned. At times the public speaker may be compelled to state ideas wholly opposed to those held by the people. Not everyone who comes to listen shares one opinion. Under such conditions, sweetness alone cannot and must not be the aim.

Some will praise, “Saraswati dances upon his tongue,” while others simultaneously mock, “What thunderous bluster the fellow makes! Throw stones at him!” The public speaker inhabits a strange city.

The same speech that appears pleasant, useful and full of knowledge to many may seem scorching, empty and devoid of wisdom to others. Each may act according to his view. Some will call it a breeze; others a storm. Some will call it sweeter than honey; others unbearable to the ear. Some will call it idle talk; others a jewel of wisdom.

One must not be preoccupied with these reactions. Those who want public speech to be responsible work and a useful instrument must not pawn their ideas to fear, pity or favour.

Nor should one mistake for a heroic call the conduct of a rough man riding a wild horse and wielding a stick. It is impossible to define with certainty the good path lying between these two prescribed routes. Each person must labour to discover that path.

To find it, speakers need deep faith in and respect for the subject on which they speak, and equal respect for and confidence in the people who come to the meeting. Rather than aiming only at sweetness, it is better and nobler to speak with confidence while respecting both the people and the principles.

Valluvar's saying, “The wound burnt by fire will heal; the wound burnt by the tongue will not,” is highly appropriate to private conversation. On the public platform, many wounds heal. The platform is a strange city full of scenes in which people who once threw stones, believing themselves scarred by the speaker's tongue, later realise, “His speech showed us the right way,” and return carrying heavy garlands in the same hands that lifted stones.

It is therefore good to choose a useful subject that does not sow the seed of pointless quarrel. If one strives to be sweet at all costs, one will eventually have to seek ankle bells and cymbals.

Even sweetness is born from firmness of principle; it does not come from surrendering principle.

For speech to conform to Valluvar's teaching, listeners too must conform to it. Valluvar, who laid down a rule for speakers, also laid down one for listeners:

> Whatever the matter, and whoever speaks it, wisdom is to discern its truth.

But in practice this Kural is absent. Tiruvalluvar said discerning truth is wisdom; conditions have made discerning truth difficult.

Speech is a collection of ideas. If speech is to provide benefit and flavour, one must attend to its ideas. An idea is the fruit of thought; thought arises from sustained interest in what one sees and hears.

The same object can stir different thoughts in different observers, prompt different ideas and produce different speeches. A neem leaf produces different thoughts in a physician and a priest.

As an example, invite several people to speak about a horse. Their speeches will be entertainingly different.

A student of history may say with relish: “Why should horses not possess majesty and greatness? Did not Alexander the Great ride the horse Bucephalus?” A reader of Tamil records will not omit the horses Parasari and Neelaveni from the story of Desingu Raja.

One who has read old verse may describe the horse Kalamegam saw: two people pulled it from the front, some pushed from behind, and it travelled only a *kadam* in a month.

Someone intent on explaining the evils of the Guindy races may rage: “Demon who cuts the marriage thread! Saturn who sets father and son against each other! Omen that traps people in intrigue! Great sinner who raises anguish between friends! Families have been ruined by you, moneylenders have grown fat, and you have set fire to households. You have even caused murder and theft. Horse, embodiment of horror! Instrument of sin! Partner in evil! May your race perish, perish root and branch, and disappear without a trace!”

A puranic storyteller may narrate the myth that Lakshmi once cursed Brahma to be born as an owl; Brahma became an owl, and from that owl's egg the horse was born.

A Saiva preacher may begin: “Horses? Your humble servant feels uncontrollable anger at the sight of them. Was it not because of these wretched horses that my Manikkavasagar was imprisoned and suffered?” Yet he may end: “On deeper reflection, compassion for the horse arises, for was it not through the horse that the land came to know Manikkavasagar's greatness? Therefore, long live the race of horses!”

If speaking about a horse can produce so many differences, will differences be few when we discuss national affairs, the plans of rulers, and ideas found in books? Public speech therefore becomes a field where many kinds of ideas move about and battle one another. A battlefield has both reward and enmity. Does it not require both an instrument and an unshaken heart?

Ideas must be gathered with an awareness of this condition. Flowers differ in colour and scent. Fragrant flowers may be made into a garland, a bouquet or a strand.

There is certainly skill in stringing a garland. But what use is displaying all one's skill by making a garland of paper flowers? The primary material of a garland is the fragrant flower. Likewise, the primary material of speech is ideas possessing flavour and benefit.

Does jasmine lose its fragrance because it blooms in an adversary's garden? We will still appreciate it. Likewise, if a speaker's idea is useful, listeners will accept it whatever their political association.

It is therefore extremely important to collect ideas for public speech and cause them to grow through thought. As one beautifully strings flowers already in bloom, one must arrange, classify and distinguish the ideas one has found, shaping speech with victorious words, clear style and a confidence-inspiring course.

Rather than saying it “must be shaped,” it may be more fitting to say one must reach the condition in which it takes shape naturally. If there are pure purpose, clear knowledge and confidence that the principle will prevail, speech that begins with stumbles and hesitation will, in time, become a resounding call.

One should not construct a speech by thinking: “I must speak like this person, throw out the word that person used, include a quotation or two, summon Emerson or Ingersoll to my aid, and insert comic fragments here and there.” Such a speech is a mixed garland: a sprig beside one jasmine, then a chrysanthemum, a little marjoram, a scentless flower, then more foliage.

If a mixed garland contains few flowers and much foliage, women will not take it. Likewise, if a speech has ideas scattered here and there but contains much that is useless, nobody will accept it. Ideas are therefore supremely important. Style is a rainbow: it does not provide beauty for long.

One must develop the desire to establish justice, uphold honesty, explain plans suited to the country, destroy ignorance, remove cruelty, and end pettiness and decay. Public speaking is the beautiful arrangement and presentation of good ideas that bloom from the thought created by that desire.

Sometimes a good speech meets an evil fate, like a flower garland thrown before a rutting elephant and trampled underfoot. At other times it reaches people capable of recognising and receiving its flavour and value, just as a flower reaches a woman who smells it, places it in her hair and delights in it, thereby gaining honour and purpose.

The path to success in public speaking is not first to worry about what may happen. It is first to believe that giving the country a speech woven from ideas is our duty. That path is common to everyone. Everyone has the right to it. Anyone who tries can succeed.

---NOTES---
The work is a complete seven-image instructional essay dated 1948, not a transcript tied to one event. Cultural examples and mythological references are retained because they demonstrate Anna's central claim that one subject yields many speeches depending on the speaker's purpose. The OCR form “மாதம் காதவழி” is understood through the Kalamegam comic-horse verse as travelling only a kadam in a month; the source remains verbatim. “Public speaking” consistently renders மேடைப் பேச்சு rather than the narrower literal “stage speech.”
